usage: refine COMMAND [options] [operands]

Commands:
  check       Check a whole program without producing artifacts
  compile     Compile a whole program to an executable or assembly
  run         Compile a whole program to a temporary executable and run it
  fmt         Format source files, or a directory's, in the one Landin layout
  svd         Generate a device's register modules and interrupts from its SVD
  bindings    Generate Landin bindings and C adapters for C headers
  explain     Explain diagnostic codes, and the rules and decisions notes cite
  site        Resolve a panic site or a caller's coordinates to a source line
  lsp         Serve the language server protocol over stdio
  version     Identify this compiler build
  targets     List described targets and their CPU feature levels
  completion  Print shell completion definitions
  help        Show help for a command or the complete CLI reference

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

Use 'refine COMMAND --help' or 'refine help COMMAND'.
--version is an alias for 'refine version'.
Bare source requests and --identify remain compatibility
spellings; use check, compile, run, version and
targets.
'build' is reserved for future project orchestration.

usage: refine check [options] source.ldn ...

Check a whole program without producing artifacts

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --target VALUE            Select a target; default is this compiler's host
  --level VALUE             CPU feature level whose instructions to use; default the baseline
  --root VALUE              Append an ordered module import root (repeatable)
  --library VALUE           Where core, hosted and platform are; default beside refine
  -L DIR                    Search DIR for linker.library's libraries (repeatable)
  --option VALUE            Override a declared NAME=VALUE build option
  --build-mode VALUE        compiler.build_mode: debug (default), release; not optimization
  --optimize VALUE          Code objective: none, size (default), speed
  --specialize VALUE        Direct evidence calls: off, auto (default, if worth it), all
  --link VALUE              Linux link: static (default) alone, or libc with C's runtime
  --firmware-entry VALUE    Select the firmware entry routine
  --firmware-layout VALUE   Firmware layout: boot (default), ram
  --build-report VALUE      Write deterministic build evidence JSON
  --stage-report VALUE      Write stage CPU time and peak memory JSON
  --warnings VALUE          Base warning selection: default, all, none
  --warn VALUE              Enable a warning code or all (repeatable)
  --allow VALUE             Suppress a warning code or all (repeatable)
  --deny VALUE              Enable and promote a warning to an error (repeatable)

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

Sources form one whole program. With --root, the operand
is one entry-module directory, or the entry module's
source files, and its imports are found under the roots.
Build mode, optimization, specialization and debug
information are independent. --build-mode changes
generated code only where source reads
compiler.build_mode.
--optimize chooses selection, inlining and register
allocation for size or speed. --specialize turns
a generic instance's evidence call into a direct one
where it proves the table; all skips auto's cost test,
and an instance whose address is taken, or a call
through any, stays indirect. --build-report lists each
call left indirect, under left_indirect, and why.
The build report's fields are listed under "The build
report" in compiler/ada/README.md.
Example: refine check program.ldn

usage: refine compile [options] source.ldn ...

Compile a whole program to an executable or assembly

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --target VALUE            Select a target; default is this compiler's host
  --level VALUE             CPU feature level whose instructions to use; default the baseline
  --root VALUE              Append an ordered module import root (repeatable)
  --library VALUE           Where core, hosted and platform are; default beside refine
  -L DIR                    Search DIR for linker.library's libraries (repeatable)
  --option VALUE            Override a declared NAME=VALUE build option
  --build-mode VALUE        compiler.build_mode: debug (default), release; not optimization
  --optimize VALUE          Code objective: none, size (default), speed
  --specialize VALUE        Direct evidence calls: off, auto (default, if worth it), all
  --emit VALUE              Artifact kind: exe (default), asm, ir, ir-annotated, linker-script
  -o, --output VALUE        Output path; default a.out, a.s or a.ir
  --keep-asm                Keep an executable's assembly beside it as OUT.s
  --debug VALUE             Debug information: none (default), full, lines
  --panic-map               Emit an off-target runtime check-site map
  --toolchain VALUE         Override the target's native compiler driver
  --linker VALUE            Select the native linker through that driver
  --link VALUE              Linux link: static (default) alone, or libc with C's runtime
  --firmware-entry VALUE    Select the firmware entry routine
  --firmware-layout VALUE   Firmware layout: boot (default), ram
  --linker-script VALUE     Link firmware with FILE, checked against the contract
  --build-report VALUE      Write deterministic build evidence JSON
  --stage-report VALUE      Write stage CPU time and peak memory JSON
  --stack-report VALUE      Write each emitted routine's frame and deepest stack JSON
  --dry-run                 Check and plan compilation; write and execute nothing
  --depfile VALUE           Write Make-format dependencies after successful emission
  --warnings VALUE          Base warning selection: default, all, none
  --warn VALUE              Enable a warning code or all (repeatable)
  --allow VALUE             Suppress a warning code or all (repeatable)
  --deny VALUE              Enable and promote a warning to an error (repeatable)

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

Sources form one whole program. With --root, the operand
is one entry-module directory, or the entry module's
source files, and its imports are found under the roots.
Build mode, optimization, specialization and debug
information are independent. --build-mode changes
generated code only where source reads
compiler.build_mode.
--optimize chooses selection, inlining and register
allocation for size or speed. --specialize turns
a generic instance's evidence call into a direct one
where it proves the table; all skips auto's cost test,
and an instance whose address is taken, or a call
through any, stays indirect. --build-report lists each
call left indirect, under left_indirect, and why.
The build report's fields are listed under "The build
report" in compiler/ada/README.md.
Example: refine compile program.ldn -o program
Example: refine compile --emit asm program.ldn -o program.s
Example: refine compile program.s -o program
An executable's assembly is the link's input and is
removed after it; --keep-asm keeps it as OUT.s, and
a .s operand is linked as a compiled program is,
with the same --target, --level and --link.
--emit ir writes the IR the backend receives.
--emit ir-annotated also records the target and
settings, module-qualified routine names and the IR's
metadata.
Hosted targets support --debug full; firmware targets
support --debug lines. Firmware executables require
--firmware-entry.
Example: refine compile --link=libc -L vendor/lib program.ldn -o program
linker.library("NAME") links libNAME, a shared
library or an archive, as the C driver's -lNAME
does; on Linux it needs --link=libc.
linker.directory("DIR") in the source, relative to
its file, and -L DIR, relative to here, say where
libraries are, -L's first; each is
also where the program looks for them when it runs.

usage: refine run [options] source.ldn ... [-- ARGUMENT ...]

Compile a whole program to a temporary executable and run it

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --target VALUE            Select a target; default is this compiler's host
  --level VALUE             CPU feature level whose instructions to use; default the baseline
  --root VALUE              Append an ordered module import root (repeatable)
  --library VALUE           Where core, hosted and platform are; default beside refine
  -L DIR                    Search DIR for linker.library's libraries (repeatable)
  --option VALUE            Override a declared NAME=VALUE build option
  --build-mode VALUE        compiler.build_mode: debug (default), release; not optimization
  --optimize VALUE          Code objective: none, size (default), speed
  --specialize VALUE        Direct evidence calls: off, auto (default, if worth it), all
  --debug VALUE             Debug information: none (default), full, lines
  --panic-map               Emit an off-target runtime check-site map
  --toolchain VALUE         Override the target's native compiler driver
  --linker VALUE            Select the native linker through that driver
  --link VALUE              Linux link: static (default) alone, or libc with C's runtime
  --firmware-entry VALUE    Select the firmware entry routine
  --firmware-layout VALUE   Firmware layout: boot (default), ram
  --linker-script VALUE     Link firmware with FILE, checked against the contract
  --build-report VALUE      Write deterministic build evidence JSON
  --stage-report VALUE      Write stage CPU time and peak memory JSON
  --stack-report VALUE      Write each emitted routine's frame and deepest stack JSON
  --warnings VALUE          Base warning selection: default, all, none
  --warn VALUE              Enable a warning code or all (repeatable)
  --allow VALUE             Suppress a warning code or all (repeatable)
  --deny VALUE              Enable and promote a warning to an error (repeatable)

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. '--' begins the program's arguments; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

Sources form one whole program. With --root, the operand
is one entry-module directory, or the entry module's
source files, and its imports are found under the roots.
Build mode, optimization, specialization and debug
information are independent. --build-mode changes
generated code only where source reads
compiler.build_mode.
--optimize chooses selection, inlining and register
allocation for size or speed. --specialize turns
a generic instance's evidence call into a direct one
where it proves the table; all skips auto's cost test,
and an instance whose address is taken, or a call
through any, stays indirect. --build-report lists each
call left indirect, under left_indirect, and why.
The build report's fields are listed under "The build
report" in compiler/ada/README.md.
Example: refine run program.ldn
Example: refine run program.ldn -- input.txt
The executable is written into a new directory only you
may enter, under TMPDIR or /tmp, and removed when the
program ends. Arguments after -- are the program's,
exactly; write a source beginning with - as
./-name.ldn. The program runs in this directory with
this terminal, and refine exits with its status, or
with 128 and the number of the signal that ended it.
Ctrl-C reaches the program through the terminal's
foreground process group. A supervisor that signals
refine alone should send SIGTERM or SIGHUP, which
refine forwards before it cleans up; SIGINT and
SIGQUIT sent to refine alone are ignored.
run runs this compiler's own host; build another
target with refine compile --target=NAME.

usage: refine fmt [options] source.ldn|DIR ...

Format source files, or a directory's, in the one Landin layout

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --check                   Report every line out of the layout; write nothing
  --stdout                  Print the one source in the layout; write nothing

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

fmt reads named files or directories, not standard input.
refine fmt --stdout source.ldn prints one source in the
layout, and an editor can format through refine lsp.

usage: refine svd --policy=POLICY.json --out-dir=DIR DEVICE.svd

Generate a device's register modules and interrupts from its SVD

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --policy VALUE            The policy that selects what is generated, and how
  --out-dir VALUE           Directory the outputs are written under

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

Example: refine svd --policy=devices/policies/rp2040.json
  --out-dir=devices RP2040.svd
The SVD must be the file the policy was reviewed against,
by its SHA-256. The policy selects the peripherals and
registers to generate and says how each may be read and
written; devices/README.md describes it. Every output is
written beside its destination and read back before any
is replaced, and a failure puts back what was replaced.
scripts/fetch_inputs.py fetches a pinned SVD by its hash.

usage: refine bindings --clang=CLANG [--sysroot=DIR] --policy=POLICY.json --out-dir=DIR [LOGICAL=]HEADER.h ...

Generate Landin bindings and C adapters for C headers

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --target VALUE            Select a target; default is this compiler's host
  --policy VALUE            The policy that selects what is generated, and how
  --out-dir VALUE           Directory the outputs are written under
  --clang VALUE             The Clang that reads the headers, by path or name
  --sysroot DIR             The target's system root; an empty one will do
  --include-dir DIR         Search DIR for quoted and angled headers (repeatable)
  --system-include-dir DIR  Search DIR for system headers (repeatable)
  --define VALUE            Define NAME, or NAME=VALUE, for Clang (repeatable)

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

Example: refine bindings --clang=clang-19 --target=linux-x86-64
  --sysroot=sysroot --policy=policy.json --out-dir=generated
  --system-include-dir="$(clang-19 -print-resource-dir)/include"
  api/public.h=include/public.h
A header is LOGICAL=PATH, or PATH, whose last name is then
its logical one: the relative name exports.h includes it
by. Clang reads the headers and nothing else: it gets
-nostdinc, the roots and defines named here, and an
environment of PATH, HOME and the temporary directory
alone. --sysroot is required for a hosted target and
refused for cortex-m0, which is freestanding: name its
Clang's own headers with --system-include-dir.
Four files are written: bindings.ldn, adapters.c,
exports.h and bindings.json; each is written beside its
destination and read back, and adapters.c compiled,
before any is replaced. The complete policy reference:
https://www.701.dev/bindings.html

usage: refine explain [options] [CODE|[NNNN]|DNNN ...]

Explain diagnostic codes, and the rules and decisions notes cite

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --library VALUE           Where core, hosted and platform are; default beside refine
  --full                    Add a construct's text or a decision's chosen rule

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

usage: refine site [options] PROGRAM SITE | PROGRAM FILE LINE COLUMN

Resolve a panic site or a caller's coordinates to a source line

Options:
  -h, --help                Print this command's help and exit
  -v, --verbose             Describe the request and name the sources read; repeat to trace tool commands
  -q, --quiet               Suppress progress, retaining warnings and errors
  --color VALUE             Diagnostic color: auto (default), always, never
  --diagnostics VALUE       Diagnostic format on standard error: human (default), short, json
  --map VALUE               The build's source table; default PROGRAM.sources.json
  --dsym VALUE              A Mach-O program's dSYM DWARF file, held to its UUID

Shared options work before or after the command. Values may use
a space or '='. Use '--' before literal operands; @FILE reads
a response file. Single-valued options may be given once.
Of the shared options, lsp takes only --help.

Example: refine site firmware.elf 4194
PROGRAM is what the compilation wrote: an executable, a
firmware image, or the assembly of --emit=asm. SITE is
the number a panic_handler receives, in decimal or
after 0x; compile with --panic-map to record sites.
FILE LINE COLUMN are a caller's three coordinates.
The answer is the source path, line and column, and
for a site the check that failed. The table beside the
program, PROGRAM.sources.json, answers only for the
build it was written with: the program's build
identity, or the assembly's SHA-256, must be the
table's. A stripped program keeps its identity.

